# Test hints for MultiService
# Multi-service example with HTTP, Socket, and File Monitor
type: multiservice
# mode: interpreter — two of the three gaps this pinned are now closed.
#
# File events were the first: the compiled binary reported FILE MODIFIED for a
# creation on macOS and reported nothing at all on Linux, because only the
# FSEvents backend ever published an event — inotify and the polling fallback
# just printed a line. Fixed in GitLab #693.
#
# The socket welcome was the second: the compiled socket *server* dispatched to
# no `Socket Event Handler` at all, so `Handle Client Connected` never ran and
# nothing was sent. Fixed in GitLab #881 — compiled, this example now sends the
# welcome, echoes through its own `Handle Data Received`, and runs its
# disconnect handler.
#
# What still pins it is GitLab #882: a compiled file monitor watches the
# process's working directory rather than the path it was given. The harness
# runs examples from the project root, so the compiled binary reports a
# FILE CREATED for every build artefact anything else happens to write:
#
#   Socket: FILE CREATED: ./.build/index-build/.../AROLSPTests~partial.swiftmodule.tmp
#
# It therefore passes only while the tree is quiet — during a full run with
# builds in flight it fails and passes on retry. Flipping to `mode: both` now
# would buy a flaky job rather than coverage.
#
# Flip to `mode: both` once #882 lands.
mode: interpreter
timeout: 20
skip-on-windows: Multi-service tests not supported on Windows CI
